WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.960
"The Green Knight" is adapted from a 14th century&nbsp;
epic poem called "Sir Gawain and the Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.498
Knight." This is a sampling of some of the biggest&nbsp;
questions the film probably left you with -- some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.498 --> 00:00:13.840
that can be answered by investigating the&nbsp;
original text, and some meant to be unanswerable.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.520
When Gawain heads off to celebrate Christmas with&nbsp;
the king and queen, his mother opts to stay home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.760
She says she isn't feeling up to partying,&nbsp;
but she's very up to casting some spells:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.840
She and three other women perform a ritual&nbsp;
which ultimately summons the Green Knight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.894
And you would be absolutely right to ask&nbsp;
why the heck she'd do that. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.894 --> 00:00:36.880
if not for this summoning, Gawain would never have&nbsp;
ended up in the situation he finds himself in.&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.295
"The Green Knight" never answers this question,&nbsp;
but "Sir Gawain and the Green Knight" does -- at&nbsp;least kind of. 

00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:46.400
The thing to remember here is who&nbsp;
the players are. The king and queen, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.640
aren't just anyone -- they are King Arthur and&nbsp;
Queen Guinevere. And Gawain's mother is Arthur's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.000
sister, making her Morgan le Fey, an enchantress&nbsp;
and not exactly a great sibling. In the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.840
text, le Fay's intention is to frighten&nbsp;
Guinevere and also prove that Arthur's knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.760
aren't as heroic as they're made out to be.
Whether those motives remain the same in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.000
Green Knight" is unclear. One would think she&nbsp;
might be upset that her son winds up connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.640
with the Green Knight in such a lethal way, but&nbsp;
she seems fine with it. Perhaps she resents her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.960
son — or perhaps she believes the quest Gawain&nbsp;
must go on thanks to the Green Knight will lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.486
to him succeeding Arthur as king. Either way,&nbsp;
we're never supplied with a definitive answer.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.160
Shortly into Gawain's quest to find the Green&nbsp;
Chapel, he comes upon a young man hovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.160
over the dead bodies from a recent battle. The&nbsp;
young man seems like he wants to help Gawain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.320
but he turns out to be a thief. Both the&nbsp;
thief and his two compatriots leave Gawain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.520
tied up while stealing both his horse&nbsp;
and his axe — the one he got from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.520
Green Knight at the beginning of the tale.
After escaping his bonds, Gawain comes upon a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.560
small cottage haunted by the ghost of its former&nbsp;
resident, Winifred, played by Erin Kellyman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.880
Winifred reveals that someone chopped her head&nbsp;
off and dumped it in the nearby lake -- she even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.720
asks Gawain if he was the one who killed&nbsp;
her. Gawain decides to help Winifred by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.880
fetching her head and returning it to the rest&nbsp;
of her dead body in the cottage. When he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.236
Winifred reveals that the Green Knight is someone&nbsp;
Gawain knows and Gawain's axe magically appears.&nbsp;

00:02:04.236 --> 00:02:08.480
Where did the axe come from? Did the thief&nbsp;
who stole the axe kill Winifred? That seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.240
unlikely, since Winifred is already&nbsp;
decayed to a full skeleton. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.301
time seems to operate very strangely in&nbsp;
"The Green Knight," so it is possible.&nbsp;

00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:20.720
Regardless, we never find out for sure who killed&nbsp;
Winifred, or how Gawain's axe finds its way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.680
into her cottage -- other than to say that maybe&nbsp;
Gawain isn't quite all there. Speaking of which...

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.560
Gawain spends much of his journey without&nbsp;
any human companionship. As we've seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.320
even when he does encounter other people,&nbsp;
things don't work out terribly well for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.160
Beaten down by the elements and starving,&nbsp;
he winds up eating some wild mushrooms;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.316
moments later, he vomits and begins to&nbsp;
imagine moss growing all over his hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.316 --> 00:02:46.240
Although the movie never states this explicitly,&nbsp;
it's a safe bet that those mushrooms were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.560
little poisonous and a lot hallucinogenic.
The real question is: how long do those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.760
hallucinations last? Furthermore, how reliable&nbsp;
a narrator is Gawain even from the start? While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.971
tied up by thieves, he seems to imagine himself as&nbsp;
a rotting skeleton that never escaped its bonds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.971 --> 00:03:05.267
That alone makes it difficult to know what's&nbsp;
real and what isn't along Gawain's quest.&nbsp;

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:10.668
And things get even more unbelievable when Gawain&nbsp;
runs into a group of naked giants roaming the&nbsp;countryside. &nbsp;

00:03:10.668 --> 00:03:13.840
He attempts to get them to carry&nbsp;
him the rest of the way to the Green Chapel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.360
Instead, they're warded off by a fox&nbsp;
who spends much of the film following,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.000
and perhaps guiding, Gawain on his journey.
Are the giants real? "The Green Knight" never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.720
answers this question. The fact that he's never&nbsp;
aided by the giants suggests that they're probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.960
just a hallucination. In fact, since Gawain&nbsp;
feeds the fox some of those wild mushrooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.013
it's possible they're both hallucinating&nbsp;
-- assuming the fox is real, of course.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.760
Weaving in and out of Gawain's journey to&nbsp;
the Green Chapel is a fox who seems to act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.000
as companion and guide. They rest in a cave&nbsp;
together, they eat those mushrooms together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.760
and, by the end of the story, the fox even&nbsp;
speaks to Gawain, cautioning him away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.267
meeting with the Green Knight.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.267
"Your doom is at hand."&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.480
Is the fox friend or foe? And&nbsp;
what does the fox represent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.560
the larger narrative of "The Green Knight"?
Foxes hold all sorts of different symbolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.480
meanings across history and cultures. They&nbsp;
can represent that a solution is at hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.400
or they can represent a need for patience.&nbsp;
Sometimes they're benevolent; sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.320
they're tricksters meant to be eyed warily.
In the context of "Sir Gawain and the Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.803
Knight," the fox is tied to a specific character&nbsp;
-- the wife of a lord Gawain encounters late in&nbsp;his travels.

00:04:15.803 --> 00:04:19.280
In the original text, Gawain stays&nbsp;
with the pair in their castle and forms a pact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.200
with the Lord: the Lord will provide Gawain&nbsp;
with the best food he captures in his hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.360
and, in exchange, Gawain will give the Lord&nbsp;
anything he gains during his stay in the castle.&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.360
During this time, the lord's wife kisses&nbsp;
Gawain two times before giving him a magical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.680
protective green belt. The Lord struggles&nbsp;
to succeed in his hunt that day -- he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.560
of course, hunting a fox. The fox is meant to&nbsp;
possess a similar guile and manipulative nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.680
as the lord's wife does attempting to bed Gawain.
In "The Green Knight," the Lord captures the fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.360
only to let it get away after Gawain&nbsp;
refuses to give up the belt. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.800
we're left open-ended over whether or&nbsp;
not the fox can be trusted in the movie.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.760
The Lord and his wife are not the only two people&nbsp;
Gawain meets during his time in the Lord's castle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.600
There is a third, ever-present figure throughout&nbsp;
this part of the film: the grey-haired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.160
perpetually blindfolded Sightless Woman. She&nbsp;
appears during meals, and, more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.697
she's present when the lord's wife eventually&nbsp;
succeeds in seducing Gawain with the green belt.&nbsp;

00:05:10.697 --> 00:05:15.200
Who is the Sightless Woman and why is she there?&nbsp;
At no point does "The Green Knight" directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.360
answer this question. However, in the poem "Sir&nbsp;
Gawain and the Green Knight," she's Morgan le&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.000
Fay -- and that's relevant to the film as well.
At the start of his journey, Gawain is given what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.400
appears to be the same green belt by his mother.&nbsp;
However, when Gawain is captured by thieves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.080
they steal the belt along with most of&nbsp;
his other belongings. He is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.301
shocked when the lord's wife somehow offers&nbsp;
what seems to be the same green belt later.&nbsp;

00:05:36.301 --> 00:05:39.280
One could hypothesize that it is&nbsp;
the same green belt, and that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.120
comes courtesy of the Sightless Woman who&nbsp;
is actually Gawain's mother in disguise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.200
We're never told that explicitly, but that&nbsp;
is one possible interpretation. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.120
both the lord's wife and Gawain's paramour&nbsp;
back home, Essel, are portrayed by Alicia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.280
Vikander -- so why couldn't his mother and the&nbsp;
Sightless Woman secretly be the same person?

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.760
Gawain has one ultimate end point for his&nbsp;
journey -- to meet the Green Knight in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.160
Green Chapel on Christmas Day a year after their&nbsp;
first encounter. We don't know why his mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.240
conjured the knight and we also don't know who&nbsp;
the Green Knight is beyond what appears to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.920
supernatural being incapable of death.
However, during Gawain's journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.320
Winifred tells him the Green Knight is someone&nbsp;
he knows. Later, the fox wards Gawain away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.880
from the Green Knight, explaining that the&nbsp;
figure is inhuman and utterly without mercy.&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.680
So who exactly is the Green Knight? The movie&nbsp;
never actually explains this. However, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.160
poem "Sir Gawain and the Green Knight," we're told&nbsp;
definitively who he is -- he's the Lord from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.322
castle, entreated and enchanted by Morgan le Fay&nbsp;
as a means to bring harm to Arthur and Camelot.&nbsp;

00:06:35.322 --> 00:06:39.360
The film adaptation gives us no such connection&nbsp;
between the Lord and the Green Knight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.040
It's possible that they're the same person,&nbsp;
but it's more likely that in this version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.047
the Green Knight isn't so much a&nbsp;
someone Gawain knows but a something.&nbsp;

00:06:47.047 --> 00:06:51.600
The Green Knight represents the horrible knowledge&nbsp;
that Gawain would make a bad knight, a bad father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.320
and a tragically unfit king. The Green Knight&nbsp;
brings Gawain this knowledge through a merciless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.542
vision so horrifying that Gawain chooses to be&nbsp;
decapitated by the Green Knight rather than live.&nbsp;

00:07:01.542 --> 00:07:06.720
Do we know who the Green Knight really is? No.&nbsp;
But we do know what he is -- Gawain's mortal fate.&nbsp;

